In 1923, an [[expedition|expedition]] in [[@mongolia|Mongolia]] found a toothless dinosaur lying on a clutch of eggs. Scientists assumed it had been caught stealing them and named it Oviraptor, "egg thief". Seventy years later, in the same desert, a close relative was found crouched over a nest of its own eggs, arms spread wide.

It looked exactly like a hen sheltering her chicks. The eggs had been its own all along. Birds sit on their eggs because [[@birds-dinosaurs|birds are dinosaurs]], and this habit is far older than birds themselves.

{{story:bird-nests}}

Each raindrop sends out all the colours, but only one colour from each drop reaches your eye. The drops sending you red are all at 42° from the point directly opposite the Sun, so together they form a circle. The drops sending you violet form a slightly smaller circle inside it.

From the ground, the land cuts off the bottom of that circle, so you see an arc. From a plane, or a high cliff with mist below, you can sometimes see a full circular rainbow. Because the angle depends on where your own eyes are, the person next to you sees light from a slightly different set of drops. Everyone sees their own rainbow.

{{story:newton-life}}

In 1930, a 19-year-old student named Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar sailed from India to England. On the long voyage he worked out sums about dying stars. He found a limit: a dead star's core heavier than about 1.4 Suns cannot settle down quietly. It must keep collapsing.

The famous [[astronomer|astronomer]] Arthur Eddington mocked the idea in public. Chandrasekhar kept working anyway. In 1983 he won the Nobel Prize in Physics, and NASA later named its Chandra X-ray Observatory after him. It still studies black holes today.

{{story:einstein}}

For long trips to Mars, carrying everything from Earth would be very heavy. So astronauts are learning to grow food. In August 2015, crew members on the [[@space-station|space station]] ate red romaine lettuce they had grown on board in a small plant chamber called Veggie. They added a little oil and vinegar first.

{{story:food-preserve}}

Since then they have grown cabbage, mustard greens, radishes and chilli peppers. In 2021 they made [[@tacos|tacos]] with the first [[@chillies|chillies]] grown in orbit. Plants also give astronauts something green to look after, which many say makes the station feel more like home.

Surprisingly, early chickens may not have been dinner. In Europe and elsewhere, [[archaeologist|archaeologists]] have found early chickens buried whole and uneaten, sometimes alongside people. They may have been prized for cockfighting, for their crowing at dawn or for religious reasons.

Chickens reached Europe about 2,800 years ago. Only in Roman times, centuries later, did eating chicken and [[@eggs|eggs]] become common there.

{{story:birds-dinosaurs}}

Big governments almost always spend more than they collect. The gap is called the deficit. To fill it, they borrow by selling bonds, which are promises to repay with [[interest|interest]]. Banks, pension funds and ordinary savers buy them.

Borrowing is not always bad. A road or a power station can help the [[economy|economy]] grow for decades, a bit like a family taking a loan for a house. But if debt grows faster than the economy year after year, interest payments start eating the budget. Economists keep a close eye on that balance.

{{story:great-depression}}

Write down every bit of money that comes in and goes out, in a notebook or a simple app. After a month, add it up. Many people are surprised by how much went on small snacks or in-game coins.

This is a [[@budgets|budget]]: a plan for money, made in advance. Families make one for groceries and school fees. Governments make one for roads and hospitals. Yours might only be a few hundred rupees or dollars, but the skill is the same.

{{story:upi-payments}}

Cricket has loved statistics for more than a century. A batting [[@averages|average]] is total runs divided by the number of times out. Most great Test batters average around 50. Australia's Don Bradman averaged 99.94, needing just 4 runs in his last innings to reach 100. He was out for a duck. No other batter with a long Test career has averaged much above 60, so statisticians rank Bradman's record among the most extreme in any sport.

Strike rate shows how fast a batter scores: runs per 100 balls. [[economy|Economy]] rate shows how many runs a bowler gives away per over. In T20 cricket, where every ball counts, teams like those in the IPL study match-ups, such as how a batter does against left-arm spin, before choosing a bowler.

{{story:maths-cricket-dls}}

After a month of fasting, Eid al-Fitr is a joyful day. Before the Eid prayer, every Muslim family who can afford it gives a small amount of food or money, called zakat al-fitr, to people in need. The idea is that everyone should be able to celebrate.

In India, it's often called Meethi Eid, "sweet Eid", because of sheer khurma, a dish of fine vermicelli cooked in milk with dates and nuts. Children receive Eidi, gifts of money from older relatives. Markets stay open late on the last nights of Ramadan, full of bangles, clothes and food.

{{story:moon-phases}}

Gilli-danda is a lesson in [[@lever-lift|levers]]. When you tap one end of the gilli, the ground acts as a pivot, and the gilli spins up like a seesaw. Hit it at the right spot and it rises nearly straight up.

The second hit is the hard part. The gilli is spinning and moving fast, and you have less than a second to swing. Good players can send it tens of metres. The angle matters too: hit it upwards at a moderate angle and it travels farthest, a bit like a javelin throw.

{{story:sport-indian-games}}

The second job of raised hair is to look scary. A frightened cat arches its back and puffs up its tail. A chimpanzee's hair stands on end before a fight. Porcupines raise their sharp quills, which are really thick, stiff hairs.

The same alarm system runs in you. When you get a fright, your body releases [[adrenaline|adrenaline]], which gets you ready to run or fight. It speeds up your heart and also triggers goosebumps. That's why a scary story told in the dark can make the hair on your arms and neck stand up, as part of the same burst of [[@stress|stress]].

{{story:music-goosebumps}}

On a national Pulse Polio day, about 23 lakh vaccinators fanned out across the country. They set up booths in villages and cities, then went door to door over the next days to find any child who had been missed. They marked houses with chalk to show which had been visited.

In one round, they could reach about 17 crore (170 million) children, more than half the population of the USA. If those numbers feel hard to picture, see our guide to [[@big-numbers|big numbers]].

{{story:big-numbers}}

Researchers at Stanford University compared how students, history professors and professional fact-checkers judged websites. Many students and professors read a page carefully from top to bottom and were often fooled by a polished design or official-sounding name.

The fact-checkers did something different. They opened new tabs and searched for the organisation or author, to see what others said about them. This is called lateral reading, reading sideways. It is the same habit historians use when they compare several [[@history-sources|sources]] before deciding what happened.

{{story:news-media}}

In October 1942, a powerful [[@cyclones-india|cyclone]] struck the coast of Midnapore and nearby districts. Storm surges flooded fields with seawater and destroyed crops and homes.

Soon after, a fungal disease called brown spot attacked rice plants across the region. The winter harvest was smaller than usual. How much smaller is still argued over. Some researchers think the crop failure was serious. Others say there was still a fair amount of rice in Bengal, but it was in the wrong hands.

{{story:cyclones-india}}

Golconda Fort stands on a [[granite|granite]] hill on the edge of the city. It was a strong fort of the Qutb Shahi [[dynasty|dynasty]], with walls running for several kilometres. Its builders designed a clever sound system. A clap at the main gate can be heard at the hilltop palace, which helped guards warn of visitors.

Golconda was also the world's best-known diamond market for centuries. Stones were mined along the Krishna River and brought here to be cut and sold. The [[@ranjit-singh|Koh-i-Noor]] and the blue Hope Diamond, now in the [[@smithsonian|Smithsonian museum]] in Washington, D.C., are both believed to have come from this region.

{{story:gems-crystals}}

In 1498, the Portuguese sailor Vasco da Gama reached Kerala by sea, the first European to sail round Africa to India. Portugal wanted control of the spice trade. In 1510, the commander Afonso de Albuquerque captured Goa from the Sultan of Bijapur.

Goa became the capital of Portugal's [[empire|empire]] in Asia. Old Goa grew into a grand city of churches, [[monastery|monasteries]] and markets, with ships sailing to Lisbon, Africa, China and Japan. The Basilica of Bom Jesus, built in 1605, still holds the remains of the missionary St Francis Xavier. The churches and convents of Old Goa are a UNESCO World Heritage Site.

{{story:words-borrowed}}

The rare [[@snow-leopard|snow leopard]] lives on Ladakh's rocky slopes, hunting wild sheep and goats. Its thick fur and long tail help it survive. In the Nubra valley, two-humped Bactrian [[@camels|camels]] walk the sand dunes, left over from the days of [[caravan|caravan]] trade with Central Asia.

{{story:snow-leopard}}

On the high Changthang plateau, nomads herd Changthangi goats. In the extreme cold, these goats grow a very fine, soft underlayer of [[@wool|wool]], which is made into pashmina shawls.

The book that changed many minds was *Maus*, by Art Spiegelman. It tells how his father, a Polish Jew, survived the [[@holocaust|Holocaust]]. In it, Jewish people are drawn as mice and Nazis as cats. The simple drawings make the true story easier to approach without hiding its horror. In 1992 it received a special Pulitzer Prize.

Graphic novels for young readers have boomed since. Raina Telgemeier's *Smile* (2010), about her teenage dental disasters, has sold millions of copies. Jerry Craft's *New Kid* is about a Black boy starting at a mostly white private school. In 2020 it became the first graphic novel to win the Newbery Medal, America's top prize for children's books.

